x86_64-linux  there is nothing to compare against: clang cross-compiling for this target has no <initializer_list> of its own to find, so it refuses the file before any name is emitted. The same reason initializer-list-construction.nonames records for the same header - cxx1 compiles it against include/initializer_list, which is this repository's, and clang against a library it has only for the host. What the case checks, that `Row r({1, 2})` and `Grid g({ {1,2}, {3,4} })` build through the initializer_list constructor, is checked by run.sh on every box.
x86_64-windows  the same: no <initializer_list> for this target, so clang refuses the file and there are no names to put beside cxx1's.
arm64-darwin  the only names that differ are std::initializer_list<T>::begin and end. clang finds libc++'s own std::initializer_list on the host, whose members are defined inline and emitted nowhere; cxx1 compiles against include/initializer_list and emits real functions for them, having no COMDAT. The same difference initializer-list-construction.nonames records. Every other name agrees.
